  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\"><em>The Economy and Meaningfulness</em> (2019) addresses the intersection of economic life and human well-being through a philosophical lens. Published in the peer-reviewed series Philosophy and Politics by Peter Lang Verlag, the work engages with questions of economic meaning and dignity that align with sustainable development priorities around decent work. The book circulates in academic libraries and shows modest but targeted institutional adoption, positioning it as a specialized contribution to philosophical economics rather than a high-citation work.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Hendrik Opdebeeck's <em>The Economy and Meaningfulness</em> sits at the convergence of economics and psychology, examining how economic systems and individual experience relate to questions of meaning and human flourishing. The work belongs to the Philosophy and Politics series (ISSN 1376-0920), a long-running academic imprint at Peter Lang that has published philosophical inquiries into ethics, democracy, and institutional life since at least 2012. This institutional placement reflects the book's scholarly positioning within European philosophical discourse.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Five years after publication, the work has not accumulated citations in tracked databases, a pattern consistent with specialized philosophical monographs that often build influence through classroom adoption and professional networks rather than rapid citation accumulation. What the record shows instead is library presence: the book is catalogued in at least four OpenLibrary holdings and indexed in WorldCat under subject headings including \"Economics, philosophy,\" \"Happiness,\" \"Well-being,\" and \"Utopias.\" These subject classifications suggest the work reaches readers interested in both normative economics and quality-of-life scholarship.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book's thematic alignment with Sustainable Development Goal 16.b (decent work and economic growth) indicates relevance to policy and sustainability conversations, though the record captures no explicit policy citations or media coverage at this time. This reflects a common pattern in philosophical scholarship: genuine policy resonance may develop gradually through professional communities and teaching curricula, channels that standard event data systems often do not capture. The book remains accessible through its publisher's official channels and is discoverable through major library networks.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">For scholars and instructors working in economic philosophy, business ethics, or the psychology of meaningful work, this monograph offers a focused intervention on a persistent question\u2014how economic life can align with human dignity and purpose. Its early circulation suggests a quieter, depth-oriented impact rather than broad adoption, a trajectory typical of books addressing fundamental rather than topical concerns.</p>",
